 | Scene Referred | bool | false | Ask the renderer for the scene-referred pipeline: everything between the lights and the display transform stays in scene units - real luminance, no `[0,1]`, no display encoding - so exposure, white balance, grading and the optical effects work on how much light is in the scene instead of on a picture already squeezed onto a monitor. Gated again by the `r_SceneReferred` cvar; off = the stock engine pipeline, byte for byte. Default off while the pipeline is being built stage by stage. See [SceneReferredSpec.md](SceneReferredSpec.md). |
 
+Stage S1 (exposure) is in. The exposure is applied to the light itself, so a stop of aperture,
+a shutter change, an ND filter or an ISO change moves the whole picture the way it does on a
+camera - lights, sun, sky, moon, emissive surfaces, particles, water and fog volumes together.
+
+**Known and deliberate breakage at this stage** (they are fixed at the root in the units and
+sweep stages, not hidden): **sun shafts** do not follow the exposure at all - their colour is
+normalised before it reaches the shader, which decouples them from the sun entirely and is a
+pre-existing engine bug; **volumetric clouds**, **SVOGI** and the **fog density** tuning have
+their own scales and stay where they are. Expect them to be visibly out of step at anything
+other than the neutral exposure.
+
