 cvar only decides whether the renderer listens.
 
+The camera's pre-exposure travels beside it on the same bus (`Global_User_SceneExposure`), as a
+linear scale in engine units. Stage S1 adds no cvar of its own: the exposure is a camera
+property, not a renderer setting, and `r_SceneReferred 0` switches all of it off at once.
+
+Two existing cvars change what they mean while the switch is on:
+
+- **`r_HDRDebug 1`** prints a scene-referred line instead of the measured-EV line - the EV100 the
+  camera actually applied, the EV the meter read (ISO 2720 incident form, C = 120*pi), and the raw
+  pre-exposure scale. The gap between the two EVs is the exposure error, which is what the
+  camera's AUTO mode drives to zero.
+- **`r_HDREyeAdaptationSpeed` / `r_HDRRangeAdaptationSpeed`** no longer affect the picture: the
+  engine's adaptation pass still runs (screen-space reflections and the film grain bind its
+  textures) but its exposure is not consumed. Use the camera's `Auto Exposure Response Time`
+  instead. `r_HDREyeAdaptationMode` likewise stops selecting anything - the scene-referred branch
+  is chosen ahead of it, which is the point of S0's D14 work.
+
